        {
          "schema_version": 2,
          "ts": "2026-10-14T02:02:51.936713Z",
          "trace_id": "6b3463742b944d86a7b9aa14f67c73e2",
          "session_id": "full-g-md-009",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My TSH is a little high but my free T4 is still in the normal range. What does that mean, what does guidance describe about treating it, and how often should it be rechecked?",
          "answer": "Please contact your clinician if your symptoms change or if you have questions about your results. Seek urgent care if you develop severe drowsiness, confusion, or chest pain.\n\nA raised TSH with a normal free T4 is the pattern the guidance calls **subclinical hypothyroidism**. TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone) is the signal the pituitary gland sends to the thyroid. Free T4 is the active thyroid hormone measured in the blood. TSH usually shifts first, when thyroid hormone is only slightly low, and free T4 shows whether hormone levels have actually fallen out of range. In your case the hormone level is still within range and only the signal is raised. (Source: Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, educational summary.)\n\nOne result is not enough to confirm this. TSH varies from day to day, is affected by recent illness, and is usually higher overnight and early in the morning. The guidance says an abnormal result should be repeated after several weeks before long-term treatment starts, and it calls treating a single abnormal value a common and avoidable error. Thyroid peroxidase (TPO) antibodies can show whether an autoimmune process may be behind the result and help estimate how likely the condition is to progress. (Same source.)\n\nOn treatment, the guidance describes broad agreement about treating when TSH stays persistently above about 10 mIU/L. For values between the upper reference limit and 10, the guidance differs. Some bodies describe a trial of treatment where symptoms, positive thyroid antibodies, or cardiovascular risk are present. Others describe monitoring alone for most people, citing trials in older adults that found no symptomatic benefit from treatment. Some bodies would also adjust the reference range upward for older adults. Pregnancy is handled separately, with a lower threshold for treatment. The passages I retrieved do not include a graded rating of how strong this evidence is, so I can't say how firm these recommendations are. One of my three searches failed, so the disagreement section rests on a single retrieval.\n\nOn rechecking, the six-to-eight-week interval in the guidance applies after starting levothyroxine or changing its dose. The retrieved material gives no recheck interval for someone not on treatment, so I can't give you one. Ask your clinician when to repeat the test.\n\nQuestions to take to your clinician:\n- Is my TSH above the reference range for my age, and by how much?\n- Should it be repeated, and when?\n- Do I have symptoms or other factors that would make treatment worth discussing?\n- Would a thyroid antibody test help?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
